course: history
lesson: 80
title: Games vs history: tech trees, barbarians, empty lands, wonders and falls
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: A strategy game is like a map of the London Underground: brilliantly useful, but the real city has bends, hills and millions of people the map leaves out; this last lesson of the block checks what Civilization-style games and Europa Universalis get right and what they squash flat.
explain: Games give you a tech tree in a fixed order, neat borders in one colour per country, «barbarians» with no culture, «empty» land to settle, wonders built by one ruler, civilizations that «fall» and vanish, and a leader who lives 6,000 years; real history had many paths, fuzzy borders, mixed empires, people living on every «empty» land, thousands of workers, peoples who carried on after their cities declined, and rulers who died like everyone else.
explain: Most of all, games hide the peasants: in farming societies eight or nine people in ten worked the land, while the game shows only kings, generals and merchants.
explain: Games are still good teachers of some things: maps and geography, trade-offs, cause and effect, and the habit of thinking «what would happen if»; Europa Universalis even ends in 1821 with no win button, a bit like real history, which has no finish line at all.
explain: So play, then check: use the game as the first page, and books, museums and the people whose history it is as the rest of the story.
fact: Real inventions depended on needs, accidents and resources, not a fixed tree; some were even forgotten, like ways of making Roman concrete in western Europe, so history could have gone differently.
fact: The word «barbarian» comes from Greek for people who spoke other languages; many peoples called barbarians had laws, trade and arts, and the Goths who sacked Rome in 410 were Christians.
fact: Most lands that empires called empty had people living there; Britain treated Australia as «terra nullius», nobody's land, until 1992, when Australia's highest court, in a case won by Eddie Mabo, said Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples could still own their land.
fact: The list of Seven Wonders was made by ancient Greek writers; of the seven, only the Great Pyramid of Giza still stands, and it was built by thousands of workers, many of them paid and fed, not by one ruler alone.
fact: Many Maya cities in the southern lowlands declined around 800 to 900, but millions of Maya people live today and speak around 30 Mayan languages.
fact: Real rulers lived ordinary lifetimes and passed power to others, and a change of ruler often changed a country's direction.
fact: History has no finish line and no winner; it keeps going, and we are part of it.
fact: Empires like the Ottoman Empire, the Habsburg lands and the British Empire included many peoples, languages and religions, not one people in one colour.
fact: For most of history borders were often fuzzy, with lands that paid tribute to two rulers or to none, until modern maps, surveys and treaties drew sharper lines.
fact: In farming societies most people, often eight or nine in ten, were farmers.
fact: Real diplomacy used letters, gifts, envoys and royal marriages; in the Amarna letters, from about 1350 BCE, kings of Egypt, Babylon and others call each other «brother» and ask for gold and gifts.
fact: Games can teach geography, trade-offs and cause and effect, but they simplify, so it is wise to check them against books and people who know the history.
exercise: Did history have to follow a fixed tech tree? | No, inventions depended on needs, accidents and resources | Yes, every country followed exactly the same order, step by step | Yes, inventions came in alphabetical order | No, nothing was ever invented
exercise: What did the Greek word behind «barbarian» first mean? | People who spoke other languages | People who had no laws of any kind | People who lived in caves in the hills | People who ate raw meat with their hands
exercise: What religion were the Goths who sacked Rome in 410? | Christian | They had no religion | Buddhist | They worshipped the Roman emperor
exercise: What did «terra nullius» claim about Australia? | That it was nobody's land | That it was the richest land | That it belonged to China | That it was an island in Europe
exercise: What did the Mabo decision of 1992 say? | Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples could hold land rights | Australia must become part of Britain again | Nobody had ever lived in Australia | All land belongs to sheep farmers
exercise: Which of the Seven Wonders still stands? | The Great Pyramid of Giza | The Colossus of Rhodes | The Hanging Gardens of Babylon | The Lighthouse of Alexandria
exercise: Who built the Great Pyramid? | Thousands of workers, many paid and fed | One pharaoh, all alone, with his own two hands | Aliens who came down from another planet | A single family working over one long summer
exercise: Did the Maya vanish when their cities declined? | No, millions of Maya live today and speak Mayan languages | Yes, all of them vanished in one day | Yes, they all left the Americas | No, because their great cities never declined or emptied at all
exercise: What really happened when a ruler died? | Power passed to others, often changing the country's direction | The same ruler kept power for thousands of years, like in the games | The country disappeared | Nothing, rulers never changed anything
exercise: Does real history have a finish line or a winner? | No, it keeps going and we are part of it | Yes, the first country to reach space wins | Yes, it ended in 1821 when the last war stopped | Yes, whichever empire is biggest at the end wins
exercise: What were empires like the Ottoman Empire really like? | Mixed, with many peoples, languages and religions | One people speaking one language and keeping one faith | Made only of soldiers | Empty apart from the sultan
exercise: What were most borders like before modern maps and surveys? | Often fuzzy and unclear | Always painted neat lines on the ground | Guarded by walls everywhere | Exactly like borders in games
exercise: In farming societies, about how many people were farmers? | Often eight or nine in ten | About one in a hundred | Nobody, food came from shops | Only kings and queens
exercise: What do the Amarna letters show about diplomacy around 1350 BCE? | Kings called each other «brother» and asked for gold and gifts | Kings only sent each other insults and threats by carrier pigeon | Kings never wrote to each other | Diplomacy was invented in 1900
exercise: What can games teach well? | Geography, trade-offs and cause and effect | Exactly what every single person in the past thought | The full truth about the past with nothing left out | Nothing useful at all, they are only for fun
test: Leo's tech tree goes in a straight line. "So history had to go like that, it's literally scripted." What's the best reply? | No; inventions depended on needs and luck, and some were even forgotten | Yes, every country in the world got the same techs in exactly the same order | Yes, God made a tech tree for each country | No, because there were no inventions before 1800
test: Max's game shows "barbarians" as screaming hordes with clubs. His mate: "Barbs were just savages, innit." What's true? | Many so-called barbarians had laws, trade and arts, and the Goths were Christians | They had no language at all | They lived only in caves | They were all one single tribe
test: In Europa Universalis, Ava sends a colonist to "uncolonised" land in Australia. Her nan, from Brisbane: "Empty, my foot, love." What does nan mean? | Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples lived there for tens of thousands of years | Australia really was empty until 1788 | Only kangaroos lived there | The game is completely right
test: Jaden's civ builds the pyramids in five turns with one click. His teacher: "Count again." What's the real story? | Thousands of workers built the Great Pyramid over many years | One pharaoh built it all by himself in a week, just like in the game | Slaves from Rome built it | It built itself from desert sand
test: Ella's game pops up: "The Maya have fallen!" Her Guatemalan friend laughs: "Bro, I'm literally Maya." What should be put right? | Maya cities declined, but millions of Maya people live and speak Mayan languages today | The Maya vanished without a trace | The Maya never existed | Ella's friend is joking, the Maya are long gone
test: Kai's game leader is still ruling after 6,000 years. "Lol, immortal king, he's built different." ("Built different" means special.) What would happen in real life? | The ruler would die, and someone else would take power and maybe change things | The ruler would live for ever | The country would vanish when he got tired | Everyone would forget who he was
test: Mia: "My civ won a science victory, GG!" ("GG" means good game.) Her teacher: "Real history has no finish line." What does she mean? | History keeps going with no winner, and we are part of it | History ended in 1821 | The first country to build a spaceship wins, and then history stops | Only scientists are part of history
test: Leo picks "England" and paints the map red. His teacher: "Real empires were mixed." Which example shows it? | The Ottoman Empire had many peoples, languages and religions | The Ottoman Empire had one language only, and everyone spoke Turkish | England has always been one colour | Empires never had more than one town
test: Sam's game borders are crisp lines. His Geordie grandad: "Howay, man, borders were proper mucky back then." ("Howay, man" means come on; "mucky" means messy.) What's true? | Many borders were fuzzy until modern maps and treaties | Borders were always painted on the ground in bright, crisp lines | Every field had a border wall | Borders did not exist at all
test: Ruby: "Where are the peasants in this game? It's all kings and generals, tho." What's the real picture? | Most people, often eight or nine in ten, were farmers | Peasants were very rare, most people were lords and knights | Kings and generals did most of the farming by themselves | Nobody farmed at all before tractors and machines came along
test: Ben's AI rival pops up: "I denounce you!" Ben: "Was real diplomacy that salty?" ("Salty" means grumpy.) What do the Amarna letters show? | Kings wrote polite letters, called each other «brother» and asked for gifts | Kings only shouted insults | Kings never talked to each other | Kings sent only armies, never letters
test: Leo: "Games teach history, so I don't need books, no cap!" His teacher: "They teach some things." Which is the wisest plan? | Use the game as a start, then check books, museums and the people whose history it is | Trust the game completely | Never play games at all | Believe whatever the loudest player says
exam: Identify the principal deficiency of the representation of land as «empty» within strategy games. | Such lands were in most instances inhabited by peoples with their own lives and rights | Such lands were in all instances wholly uninhabited until the arrival of European settlers | Such lands were inhabited solely by animals | Such lands did not exist prior to the game
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the circumstances of the Maya following the decline of their southern cities around the ninth century? | Their descendants persisted, numbering millions and speaking Mayan languages to this day | They disappeared entirely during the ninth century and left behind no descendants of any kind | They were transported to Europe | Their cities never declined in any respect
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the composition of the population in agrarian societies. | The great majority, frequently eight or nine persons in ten, were engaged in farming | The majority were monarchs and military commanders | Agriculture was conducted solely by machinery | Farmers constituted fewer than one person in a hundred, the remainder being artisans and merchants
exam: Ascertain the response most consistent with a judicious use of historical strategy games. | They may serve as an introduction, to be verified against books and knowledgeable persons | They constitute a complete and accurate record of the past | They possess no value whatsoever | They should supersede all historical scholarship
